## Decision
**(a) The session-close retrospect is STANDING. This changes ADR 0030 in place.** Do not do the
retrospect for each PR. You can do the retrospect at any time. The end of the session is a defined
trigger for the retrospect. The closeout mode has its home in `retrospect/SKILL.md`. Its scope is
all work that you shipped after the session started. You must use the checklist for friction. The
checklist has these items: corrections, wrong guesses, work that you did again, permission
denials, and CI failures. A result with no findings is valid. Record each finding as a diff, an
issue, or an ADR. Do not record a finding as a line that only says that all is satisfactory.

**(b) Do the two-why check at each failure point.** Before you commit the repair for a failure of
a gate, CI, or verifier, answer these questions:
1. Is this failure one instance, or is it a class of failures?
2. Which rung of the detection ladder (ADR 0047) must find this failure earlier? Why did that
   rung not find it?

The answer "operator error" does NOT complete the check. The home of this rule is the Muda block
in CLAUDE.md. This rule is an instruction. It is not a forcing function. If failures occur again
and nobody answers the questions, move the rule to a higher rung.
